Working of partition-wise data storage
 - Range partitioning by Price: p0 = below 1000 (budget items), p1 = 1000 to 9999, p2 = 10000 to
   49999, p3 = 50000 and above.
 - On INSERT, MySQL checks the Price value and stores the row in the matching partition. Each
   partition is a separate storage unit of the same logical table.
 - Advantages of partition-wise retrieval:
 - Faster queries: a query on one price band reads only that partition (partition pruning) and not the
   whole table.
 - Less I/O and better use of indexes, because each partition and its index is smaller.
 - Easy management: old or unused partitions can be dropped or backed up separately (ALTER
   TABLE Product DROP PARTITION p0).
 - Parallel work: different partitions can be read or maintained at the same time.
 - Hash alternative: PARTITION BY HASH(Product_ID) PARTITIONS 4 spreads rows evenly
   when no natural range exists.
Conclusion: partitioning splits one big table into smaller physical parts by a rule on a column. The
user still writes normal queries on Product, MySQL decides which partition stores each row, and
queries that name a partition (or filter on Price) read only that part.


