Q. 8. Write minimum 10 complex queries on MongoDB collections using suitable
query operators, projection, sorting and logical conditions.
Collection used: company.employees (7 documents from Q3). Start with: use company

Query 1: Comparison $gt with projection: salary above 50000
  db.employees.find(
    { salary: { $gt: 50000 } },
    { _id: 0, name: 1, salary: 1 }
  )

Output:
  { name: 'Ravi Patil', salary: 55000 }
  { name: 'Amit Joshi', salary: 80000 }
  { name: 'Rahul Deshmukh', salary: 60000 }
  { name: 'Neha More', salary: 52000 }

Query 2: Logical $and: IT employees earning 55000 or more
  db.employees.find(
    { $and: [ { department: 'IT' }, { salary: { $gte: 55000 } } ] },
    { _id: 0, name: 1, department: 1, salary: 1 }
  )

Output:
  { name: 'Ravi Patil', department: 'IT', salary: 55000 }
  { name: 'Rahul Deshmukh', department: 'IT', salary: 60000 }

Query 3: Logical $or: HR employees or Developers
  db.employees.find(
    { $or: [ { department: 'HR' }, { designation: 'Developer' } ] },
    { _id: 0, name: 1, designation: 1, department: 1 }
  )

Output:
  { name: 'Ravi Patil', designation: 'Developer', department: 'IT' }
  { name: 'Amit Joshi', designation: 'Manager', department: 'HR' }
  { name: 'Rahul Deshmukh', designation: 'Developer', department: 'IT' }
  { name: 'Karan Singh', designation: 'Clerk', department: 'HR' }

Query 4: Logical $nor: not in IT and not below 50000
  db.employees.find(
    { $nor: [ { department: 'IT' }, { salary: { $lt: 50000 } } ] },
    { _id: 0, name: 1, department: 1, salary: 1 }
  )

Output:
  { name: 'Amit Joshi', department: 'HR', salary: 80000 }
  { name: 'Sneha Kulkarni', department: 'Finance', salary: 50000 }
  { name: 'Neha More', department: 'Finance', salary: 52000 }

Query 5: $in: designation is Developer or Analyst
  db.employees.find(
    { designation: { $in: ['Developer', 'Analyst'] } },
    { _id: 0, name: 1, designation: 1 }
  )

Output:
  { name: 'Ravi Patil', designation: 'Developer' }
  { name: 'Rahul Deshmukh', designation: 'Developer' }
  { name: 'Neha More', designation: 'Analyst' }

Query 6: Projection that hides a field: Finance employees without contact
  db.employees.find(
    { department: 'Finance' },
    { _id: 0, contact: 0, emp_id: 0 }
  )

Output:
  {
      name: 'Sneha Kulkarni',
      designation: 'Accountant',
      department: 'Finance',
      salary: 50000
  }
  { name: 'Neha More', designation: 'Analyst', department: 'Finance', salary: 52000 }

Query 7: sort() descending on salary
  db.employees.find({}, { _id: 0, name: 1, salary: 1 })
    .sort({ salary: -1 })

Output:
  { name: 'Amit Joshi', salary: 80000 }
  { name: 'Rahul Deshmukh', salary: 60000 }
  { name: 'Ravi Patil', salary: 55000 }
  { name: 'Neha More', salary: 52000 }
  { name: 'Sneha Kulkarni', salary: 50000 }
  { name: 'Priya Shah', salary: 45000 }
  { name: 'Karan Singh', salary: 30000 }

Query 8: sort on two fields with skip() and limit()
  db.employees.find({}, { _id: 0, name: 1, department: 1, salary: 1 })
    .sort({ department: 1, salary: -1 })
    .skip(2).limit(3)

Output:
  { name: 'Amit Joshi', department: 'HR', salary: 80000 }
  { name: 'Karan Singh', department: 'HR', salary: 30000 }
  { name: 'Rahul Deshmukh', department: 'IT', salary: 60000 }

Query 9: $regex: names starting with R
  db.employees.find(
    { name: { $regex: '^R' } },
    { _id: 0, name: 1 }
  )

Output:
  { name: 'Ravi Patil' }
  { name: 'Rahul Deshmukh' }

Query 10: $exists on a nested field: employees without an email
  db.employees.find(
    { 'contact.email': { $exists: false } },
    { _id: 0, name: 1, contact: 1 }
  )

Output:
  { name: 'Karan Singh', contact: { phone: '9876500007' } }

Query 11: countDocuments(): salary between 50000 and 60000
  db.employees.countDocuments(
    { salary: { $gte: 50000, $lte: 60000 } }
  )

Output:
  4

Query 12: Aggregation $group: employee count and total salary per department
  db.employees.aggregate([
    { $group: { _id: '$department',
                employees: { $sum: 1 },
                totalSalary: { $sum: '$salary' } } },
    { $sort: { totalSalary: -1 } }
  ])

Output:
  { _id: 'IT', employees: 3, totalSalary: 160000 }
  { _id: 'HR', employees: 2, totalSalary: 110000 }
  { _id: 'Finance', employees: 2, totalSalary: 102000 }

Conclusion: comparison, logical, projection, sorting, regex, element and aggregation operators
together let us build complex MongoDB queries on the employees collection.


